<DocsLayout title="CLI notifications" activeNav="/cli/integrations/notifications">
  <h1>Notifications</h1>
  <p>
    Notification destinations send build events to a chat channel — Slack, Discord, Telegram —
    with per-event message templates.
  </p>

  <h2>There is no CLI command, and no API</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Notification destinations are configured in the web UI only.</strong> They are not
    part of API v1: <code>routes/api.php</code> defines no notification endpoints at all. Their
    routes live in the session-authenticated web application
    (<code>/integrations/notifications</code>), which an API key cannot reach.
  </p>
  <p>
    This means there is nothing for <code>appmctl</code> to call — not even through
    <code>appmctl request</code>, which is scoped to <code>/api/v1/&#123;team&#125;/</code> and
    authenticates with a bearer token. Attempting it fails on authentication, not on the path:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`# Does not work. There is no such API endpoint.
appmctl request GET integrations/notifications
# 404 Not Found`} />
  <p>
    Configure destinations at <strong>Integrations → Notifications</strong> in the web UI. What
    you set there — the channel credentials, the subscribed events, the templates, and the tag
    filters — is stored on the destination and applies to every matching event, whether the build
    was created by the CLI or not.
  </p>

  <h2>Use webhooks for automation</h2>
  <p>
    Everything a notification destination reacts to is also available as a
    <a href="/cli/integrations/webhooks">webhook</a> event, and webhooks are fully
    manageable from the CLI. If you need notifications under version control or created by a
    script, send the events to your own receiver and post to chat from there:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl webhooks create \\
  --name 'Build notifier' \\
  --url https://hooks.acme.example/build-notifier \\
  --events '["build.created","build.completed"]' \\
  --app customer-app`} />
  <p>
    That trades the built-in templates for control: your receiver decides the message format, and
    the endpoint's configuration lives wherever you keep infrastructure code. The tradeoff is
    that you now run and monitor that receiver.
  </p>

  <h2>Checking what is configured</h2>
  <p>
    Notification destinations hang off integrations, so the integration itself is visible from the
    CLI even though the destination is not. A <code>slack</code>, <code>discord</code>, or
    <code>telegram</code> provider in this list means a notification channel exists:
  </p>
  <CodeBlock lang="bash" code={`appmctl --json integrations list |
  jq -r '.data[] | select(.provider == "slack" or .provider == "discord" or .provider == "telegram")
         | "\\(.provider)  \\(.name)"'`} />
  <p>
    The destination's own settings — channel, events, templates, failure count — are not exposed.
    Read them in the web UI.
  </p>

  <h2>If this needs to be scriptable</h2>
  <p>
    Making destinations CLI-manageable is a platform change, not a CLI one. It needs API v1
    routes, a controller, a resource, an ability scope alongside the existing ones, and tests —
    at which point a <code>appmctl notifications</code> command becomes straightforward. Until
    that exists, this page describes the whole story: web UI for destinations, webhooks for
    automation.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
